  "title": "US Economics Analyst: The Fed Task Forces",
  "type": "economics",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-07-21",
  "insights": [
   "Fed Chairman Kevin Warsh has established five task forces to review Fed communications, balance sheet policy, data reliance, AI/productivity impacts, and inflation frameworks.",
   "The report anticipates limited structural changes, noting that while Warsh has significant power to alter communications, the FOMC has historically struggled to reach consensus on major shifts like eliminating the Summary of Economic Projections (SEP).",
   "A potential compromise on communications includes adopting Don Kohn's proposal to stop publishing the median projection in the SEP to avoid the appearance of an official endorsement.",
   "The balance sheet task force is unlikely to abandon the ample-reserves regime, though it may explore shifting asset holdings toward Treasury bills to minimize profit volatility.",
   "Alternative data (e.g., credit card/payroll data) will likely remain a complement to official statistics rather than a replacement due to issues with representativeness and seasonal adjustment.",
   "The Fed is expected to remain skeptical of using AI productivity forecasts to set current monetary policy, given the historical difficulty in forecasting productivity growth.",
   "Warsh's focus on monetary aggregates (M2) is viewed as unlikely to shift the Fed's primary reliance on price-based financial conditions measures, though Divisia indices may be explored."
